n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1583749884/4/codegen_prompts/2.md:22:    - 如果两者之间没有桥梁，则用 Maroon 填充两者之间的整个矩形间隙区域。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/codegen_logs_20260619_141339_seed174941624_resume_gpt54_xhigh/10_5.log:2813:- `Input structure`：背景为黑色。网格中有若干由同一种非黑色颜色（围栏色，例如 Maroon）构成的封闭矩形围栏，每个围栏是单像素宽的矩形环。每个围栏的边上有一个或多个单像素缺口（\"门\"），缺口处的围栏像素缺失，露出黑色背景。围栏内部全为黑色背景（空心）。围栏之间互不接触。另外，每个围栏内部的某个角落处有一个彩色像素（非黑色、非围栏色），该像素的颜色是该围栏的\"射线颜色\"。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/2/codegen_prompts/6.md:11:<new_puzzle index=\"6\">\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1852178230/15/codegen_prompts/4.md:18:  - 每条路径段有两个端点（即路径的两端像素），一个端点标记为 Maroon（入口端），另一个端点保持原色（出口端）。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1852178230/15/codegen_prompts/4.md:23:  - 从第二条路径段开始，将其平移并（如有必要）旋转 0/90/180/270 度，使其 Maroon 入口端恰好位于前一条路径段出口端的相邻格子（四方向相邻）。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1852178230/15/codegen_prompts/4.md:25:  - 拼接完成后，将所有 Maroon 入口端像素恢复为其路径段的原始颜色。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1852178230/15/codegen_prompts/4.md:27:- `Key insight`：左侧图例编码了拼接的顺序，Maroon 标记编码了每段的方向性（哪端是入口），解题者需要同时发现三层规则：图例定顺序、Maroon 定朝向、旋转使首尾相接。仅发现其中任一层都无法完整还原输出。\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed168776016/16/codegen_prompts/6.md:11:<new_puzzle index=\"6\">\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/_codegen_logs/run_20260619_141319_gpt54_high_resume_missing/16__codegen_prompts__8.log:30:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/16/prompt.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\\n\\n每个参考题描述包含：\\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\\n\\n参考题目如下：\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"1\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"5545f144\\\" lines=\\\"191-207\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove a triangular sprite across the grid, consuming aligned marker pixels.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- Frames concatenated horizontally with solid divider columns; bg is most common color.\\n- Each frame: a triangular arrow-shaped sprite and scattered single-pixel markers, all one color.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Start from the rightmost frame.\\n- At each step, exactly one cardinal direction from the head has reachable\\n  markers (on a half-line: same row or column); the sprite advances to the\\n  nearest one.\\n- On reaching a marker, consume it; sprite re-faces that direction.\\n- Repeat until no markers remain; output the final sprite position on a clean bg.\\n\\nKey insight: The triangle's tip encodes facing direction, which sets marker-consumption priority.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"2\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"5ecac7f7\\\" lines=\\\"23-36\\\">\\n<description>\\nCombine the left, middle, and right ordered objects from three separated panels.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid is three HxH panels separated by one-cell vertical separator bars.\\n- The background is the dominant color, and each panel contains exactly three same-color 4-connected foreground objects.\\n\\nRule\\n- Build a new HxH background canvas.\\n- From the left panel copy its leftmost object, from the middle panel copy its middle object, and from the right panel copy its rightmost object.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The separator bars create three aligned layers, and the panel's own left-to-right object order selects which object survives in the merged output.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"3\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"42f14c03\\\" lines=\\\"75-83\\\">\\n<description>\\nCrop the foreground object whose missing cells match marker shapes, and fill those missing cells with the marker colors.\\n\\nInput structure\\nA dominant background surrounds several monochromatic foreground objects. Exactly one object has background gaps inside its bounding box whose component shapes are matched by separate marker objects.\\nRule\\nFind that object, crop its bounding box, and replace each background gap component with the color of the separate marker component or components having the same normalized shape.\\nKey insight\\nThe marker objects do not mark absolute positions; their shapes identify which gaps in the selected object should be colored, while objects with unmatched gaps are distractors.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"4\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"88e364bc\\\" lines=\\\"102-115\\\">\\n<description>\\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\\n\\nRule:\\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\\n\\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"5\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"3dc255db\\\" lines=\\\"37-53\\\">\\n<description>\\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\\n\\nRule:\\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\\n\\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"6\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"6d1d5c90\\\" lines=\\\"8-23\\\">\\n<description>\\nRemove the left control column and roll the remaining image downward by the red marker's row.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The input is a rectangular grid with a one-cell-wide control column on the left.\\n- That control column is all pink except for exactly one red marker pixel.\\n- The columns to the right are the image that remains in the output.\\n\\nRule\\n- Delete the left control column.\\n- Let r be the row index of the red marker in that column.\\n- Cyclically shift the remaining image downward by r rows.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The red marker's vertical position encodes the row-roll amount; the pink\\n  column is only a control strip and is removed after reading it.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"7\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"8f3a5a89\\\" lines=\\\"22-36\\\">\\n<description>\\nPaint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.\\n- A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.\\n- Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.\\n- Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).\\n- Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.\\n\\nKey insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"8\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"7acdf6d3\\\" lines=\\\"25-42\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove the source-color pixels into the unique outline interior whose size matches the source count.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid has a dominant background and exactly two foreground colors.\\n- One foreground color forms 8-connected outline objects with row-wise interior cells.\\n- The other foreground color supplies a set of source pixels away from the chosen outline.\\nRule\\n- For each possible outline color, compute each 8-connected outline object's\\n  row-span interior: background pixels strictly between the leftmost and\\n  rightmost outline pixels on each occupied row.\\n- Select the unique outline interior whose cell count equals the total\\n  number of pixels of the other foreground color.\\n- Erase the source pixels and paint that matched interior with the source color.\\nKey insight\\n- The colors are roles, not fixed IDs: the source color is identified by\\n  counting, and the destination is the outline with a matching interior area.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"9\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"50c07299\\\" lines=\\\"8-21\\\">\\n<description>\\nMove the diagonal foreground segment to the next longer run along the same up-right diagonal.\\n\\nInput structure\\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color. The foreground pixels form a\\ncontiguous one-pixel-wide segment on a single anti-diagonal.\\n\\nRule\\nErase the current segment. If its columns are c0..c1 and its length is L, draw the same\\ncolor on columns c1+1..c1+L+1 along the same anti-diagonal.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe segment advances past its current upper-right end by one whole segment position, and\\nthe next segment is one pixel longer than the current one.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"10\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"3cd86f4f\\\" lines=\\\"6-18\\\">\\n<description>\\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\\n\\nInput structure\\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\\n\\nRule\\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"11\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"7b3084d4\\\" lines=\\\"253-266\\\">\\n<description>\\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\\n\\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"12\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"22425bda\\\" lines=\\\"58-76\\\">\\n<description>\\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\\n\\nInput structure\\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\\n\\nRule\\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\\ngeometrically cross.\\n\\nKey insight\\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\\nbottom layer to the top layer.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"13\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"4c7dc4dd\\\" lines=\\\"7-21\\\">\\n<description>\\nLearn a transformation rule from a training pair of framed sprites, then apply it to a test sprite.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- Grid contains several colored square frames (hollow borders of a single color).\\n- Each frame's interior holds a small pattern sprite; one frame's interior is blank (all black).\\n- Two frames form a training pair (their interiors share the same color palette); one remaining frame is the test case.\\n\\nRule:\\n- Identify the training pair by matching interior color sets; the other non-blank frame is the test input.\\n- Infer the rule mapping source→destination (e.g. reflection, color inversion, gap filling, endpoint extraction, or checkerboard complement).\\n- Apply that same rule to the test sprite; output the transformed interior.\\n\\nKey insight: The puzzle is an analogy — \\\"A is to B as C is to ?\\\" — where you must discover the visual rule from one example pair and generalize it.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"14\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"7e2bad24\\\" lines=\\\"91-106\\\">\\n<description>\\nExtend the diagonal blue ray through the grid, reflecting it off red line-segment mirrors.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- Black-background grid with one diagonal 8-connected blue ray seed.\\n- Red foreground objects, when present, are straight horizontal or vertical mirror segments.\\n- Other foreground bars are transparent to the ray and may be overwritten where the ray crosses them.\\n\\nRule\\n- Start from the ray seed endpoint whose outward diagonal extension remains inside the grid.\\n- Paint the ray continuously in that diagonal direction until it leaves the grid.\\n- If the next cell is a red mirror, do not paint it; reflect by flipping the row direction on a horizontal mirror or the column direction on a vertical mirror.\\n\\nKey insight\\n- The non-red bars are decoys: only red bars reflect the diagonal path, while all other crossed cells become blue.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"15\\\" source=\\\"train\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"2a28add5\\\" lines=\\\"8-22\\\">\\n<description>\\nPaint each pink control pixel as a teal horizontal bar whose reach equals the foreground counts on that row.\\n\\nInput structure\\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one or more pink control pixels.\\n- Each marker row has exactly one pink pixel; all other non-background pixels\\n  in that row are counters.\\nRule\\n- Start from an all-background output grid.\\n- For each pink pixel, count non-background pixels strictly to its left and\\n  strictly to its right in the same row.\\n- Paint teal from c - left_count through c + right_count on that row.\\nKey insight\\n- The foreground pixels are not copied; only their counts on each side of\\n  each pink marker encode the teal bar length.\\n</description>\\n</reference_puzzle>\\n\\n<reference_puzzle index=\\\"16\\\" source=\\\"eval\\\" puzzle_id=\\\"8698868d\\\" lines=\\\"9-23\\\">\\n<description>\\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\\n\\nInput structure:\\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\\n\\nRule:\\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\\n\\nKey insight: The matching key is the numbe�层推理。\\n   - ✓ 真两层：\\\"通过孔洞数量匹配颜色图例\\\"→\\\"用匹配到的颜色重绘对象\\\" — 两步都需要非平凡的归纳。\\n\\n   如果你发现自己写出的题只是\\\"对每个对象，根据它的某个属性做某变换\\\"的单步规则，请引入一个需要主动推理的前置步骤。**用堆叠 tie-break 规则或复杂数学公式来\\\"加难度\\\"是劣解；用多一层需要归纳的结构嵌套来\\\"加难度\\\"才是好解**。\\n10. 在符合ARC题目和提示词要求的基础上，鼓励尝试创新规则为多层结构的题目，不局限于两层，可以创新三层或四层结构的题目，但不可为了多结构层数违背要求。\\n\\n生成后对生成的题执行以下四项审查（审查内容不需要保存到文件）：\\n1. 原创性\\n  新题不可与上述参考题相同或类似，不可只是替换颜色、方向、对象类型等表层要素。判断标准：新题的核心推理路径应与任何一道参考题不同。\\n2. 确定性\\n  规则完全确定，同一输入只能产生唯一输出，每一步操作都有明确定义。\\n3. 可实现性\\n  所有必要细节都已给出——对象如何生成、如何识别、如何变换、边界情况如何处理，读完描述后可以直接写代码。\\n4. ARC 合规性\\n  网格尺寸合理（≤30×30），使用颜色不超过 10 种，规则复杂度适中，人类能从 3-5 个样例中发现规律。没有使用上述注意事项第二条禁止使用的复杂计算。\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/10/7.py:85:    - Black background with one or more Maroon U-shaped rectangular fences.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/10/7.py:87:    - The interior of each fence contains several single colored pixels, none of which are Black or Maroon.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/10/7.py:90:    - Identify each Maroon fence and determine which side is open.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed56627177/10/7.py:93:    - Leave the Maroon fences unchanged and clear every fence interior cell to Black.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/codegen_logs_20260619_1